  "reviewedScope": "On the delivered 614 bindless array-delta Core: DMA copies of R16G16B16A16Float and R32G32B32A32Float textures use the GPU-side copy for Hogwarts Legacy only (GraphicsConfig.HogwartsFloatDmaGpuCopy, set per session by the Library from the launched title; every other title keeps the MeloNX exclusion), into a block-linear destination with the same element size and a target sized in bytes; a destination texture that cannot be created falls back to the copy through guest memory (all formats). Every 2D copy still read through guest memory is attributed by bounded logging (each signature once, at most 8 per reason; counts per reason every 1024 copies, at most 64 times; no allocation per copy once full). For Hogwarts Legacy only (GraphicsConfig.HogwartsDmaRegionUpload, set with the float policy), a 2D copy from linear memory into a block-linear destination with identity components and one element size, whose destination surface is a single-level 2D uncompressed colour texture of the cache (the lookup of the direct upload of complete copies, at each width the GOB-aligned surface holds; ambiguous: none) at scale 1 without host format conversion, and whose region falls on whole texels inside it, without overlaps of an incompatible layout, is uploaded into that host texture region (rows packed as the host reads them) after the texture's memory synchronisation; guest memory is then patched as before but written untracked, so the texture and guest memory hold the same region, the texture is neither read back nor uploaded again, and its modified state is left as it was. Uploads, declines and guest-memory accesses over 2 ms are counted in the bounded summary (at most 64 lines). For Hogwarts Legacy only (the same policy), a 2D copy from linear memory whose source a GPU-modified linear texture alone holds (TextureGroup.AnyHandleModified; the copy's stride, single-level 2D uncompressed colour at scale 1, the whole source in one physical region, no other texture group over it, no incompatible overlap, the rectangle on whole texels inside the stride) into the texture of the destination surface with the same UNORM format unconverted by the host (another group, no incompatible overlap); under the policy the guest path reads its destination tracked unless the region was uploaded, is copied on the GPU (host region copy queued after the GPU writes, destination marked modified, as the upstream GPU copy of block-linear sources does) instead of reading the source through guest memory, which runs the source texture's flush action; TextureCache.FindOverlapsInRange (under the texture read lock) finds the source. Every slow source read is attributed to the overlapping textures (kind counted; each new signature logged once, at most 8). Build 621: for Hogwarts Legacy only, a 2D copy whose source is a block-linear surface that a GPU-modified texture of the cache is (the region-upload lookup; single-level 2D uncompressed colour at scale 1, no incompatible overlap, the rectangle on whole texels) is copied on the GPU (TryCopyGpuRegion) into the texture of a block-linear destination surface, found, or created over that surface as the delivered GPU copy of complete copies creates its target only when no texture of the cache overlaps the surface; same format, unconverted by the host, another group; both synchronised, the region copied by the host, the destination marked modified. The region lookup takes only a texture that starts at the surface base. Build 623: a read-back (a block-linear source region into linear memory) is deferred: copied on the GPU (TryReadBackGpuRegion) into a linear texture over the destination, found with the same layout and format or created when no texture overlaps it, without writing guest memory; a strict host sync is forced after the copy and a CPU read flushes the texture after it. Only whole contiguous rows on 4 bytes with no buffer of the cache over the destination (BufferCache.HasBufferInRange, a read-only query) qualify. The DMA summary counts read-back candidates, deferred ones, declines by reason and the first-access flushes of read-back textures (TextureGroup.ReadBackTarget: number, total and longest duration, GPU-thread ones). A byte model checks immediate, next-frame, partial and rewrite-before-read accesses against the reference copy with the real LayoutConverter. ThreadedRenderer times each command it runs by type and logs, every 5 s of rendering, the 8 command types with the most time (calls and milliseconds) to the core log. The Vulkan backend times on the render thread, for every title, each draw preparation (RenderStageTimer: the whole of RecreateGraphicsPipelineIfNeeded, now PrepareGraphicsDraw behind a timed wrapper, with its pipeline creation or lookup, barrier flush, descriptor update and auto-flush parts), each render pass begun (and those begun again on the framebuffer the last one ended on), each buffer fence wait (BufferHolder: before a direct write or for a read, on the render thread apart from other threads), each command buffer submission (FlushCommandsImpl, now SubmitCommands behind a timed wrapper, with its ReturnAndRent part) and each presentation (whole, image acquisition, queue present; Window.Present has no early return), and logs them every 5 s of presentation to the core log, then counts again; nothing else in these paths changes. Build 625: a read-back whose destination a buffer of the cache holds (build 624: 747 of 751, a whole 1x1 R16G16B16A16Float texture about once per frame) is copied on the GPU into that buffer when it covers the whole source texture and no texture overlaps the destination (BufferCache.TryCopyTextureToBuffer: ITexture.CopyTo of the level into the buffer range, the range marked GPU-modified as a buffer to buffer copy does), followed by a forced host sync; a partial region keeps the guest path and is counted. Build 626: when the only texture over that destination is the linear read-back target the first copies created (TextureGroup.ReadBackTarget, same layout and format), the copy goes into the buffer and into that texture, so both hold the latest bytes (build 625: 0 read-backs into buffers, 286 and 292 declined as occupied). Build 628: textures over that destination no longer decline the copy into the buffer; they get a write signal without a write, buffers exempt (PhysicalMemory.SignalTextureWrite), as a guest write there would give them; a buffer texture is left alone and a texture of the source group still declines (build 626: 0 read-backs into buffers, 352 and 742 declined as occupied). The first textures there are logged. Build 628, MoltenVK on a tile-based GPU (Hogwarts Legacy only, MoltenVkPolicy): the write-to-read barrier of a texture bound inside a render pass it is not an attachment of is deferred to the next begin of that pass (its forced fence), because MoltenVK restarts the Metal render pass for every GENERAL-layout image barrier recorded inside one and Metal's hazard tracking already orders the read after the attachment use of an ended pass (PipelineBase.TryDeferInPassReadBarrier, TextureStorage.QueueWriteToReadBarrier). A detail line gives the commands that ended the passes begun again, those barriers, the compute descriptor updates by program size, the first-chance exceptions and the GPU busy time from the Metal command buffers (MetalGpuTimer: -[MTLCommandBuffer commit] of the class of MoltenVK's queue replaced through the Objective-C runtime, the original always called). RenderStageTimer also times each compute dispatch preparation (pipelines, barriers, descriptors) and counts the render passes dispatches end. MoltenVK performance tracking is on (MVKConfiguration.PerformanceTracking, statistics logged every 300 presented frames to stderr). The DMA fixture runs the real RenderStageTimer with a logger double, and the fragmented-read fixture compiles the Vulkan backend from the final source. Both GPU copies take UNORM, sRGB and float formats (exact at their own size). Under the policy the guest path signals its source read and tracked destination read precisely (PhysicalMemory.SignalPreciseRead), and texture handles ignore a precise read outside their own bytes. For every title on Apple platforms, ThreadedRenderer raises the render and GPU threads, and the demand pipeline compile thread raises itself, to user-interactive QoS (a failed call logged once); the pipeline cache budget is 1 GiB (main 768 MiB, workers 128 MiB). Bindless delta passes skip the verification scan when no texture was invalidated during the pass (Texture.InvalidationEpoch) and visit only the pool ids logged since their last pass when that set is exact (TexturePool change log); otherwise the full scan or rebuild as before. The array-delta, fragmented-read (complete final GPU assembly), binding-snapshot and collector fixtures and the new DMA fixture rerun on the final source. Payload, SDK, ABI, source pin, smoke and all other lots unchanged; no physical gameplay, device allocation or FPS claim.",
